+### Registration and channel model
+
+Registration is a hard gate. It uses the existing visible-content ORB/RANSAC path and, when applied, a content-only ECC affine refinement composed with the homography before one resampling of the original capture. Injected geometry is never evidence. Same-geometry pages use the content-similarity guard. A rejected wrong-source page remains inconclusive with `hard_gate_passed=false`; if no warp existed, `overlap_fraction=null` rather than a fabricated measurement.
+
+Global channel selection evaluates the fixed blur list `[0.0, 0.6, 1.0, 1.4]`, then performs a bounded, standardized robust gain/offset fit and records rank, condition, retained samples, residual median loss, residual MAD, and fit correlation. The local model marginalizes the nine fixed subpixel translations using content-only residual posteriors, conditional on the single globally selected blur. It does **not** marginalize blur uncertainty. This is a disclosed approximation to joint blur/registration uncertainty, not a claim of full marginalization.
+
+For the final clean digital artifact the selected global fit was sigma 0, gain `1.0006703810`, offset `-0.0520655244`, median loss `0.3965851556`, residual MAD `0.6019112749`, standardized condition `1.0`, and `fit_correlation=0.9999518193`. That correlation describes the fitted source/capture coordinate relation; it is not measured true-carrier survival, BER, or evaluator-labelled recovery.
+
+The preparation reports rendered all-zero/all-one endpoint separation, not measured physical signal amplitude. Clean endpoint medians by source-luma bin were 23.919 (2 symbols), 22.196 (690), 18.012 (2,501), and 18.012 (49,307). Actual carrier amplitude by tone and BER require evaluator-only labels and remain evaluation-stage measurements. The recipient-blind inferred-coordinate-versus-bias-expectation correlation was `-0.0008927`; it is labelled accordingly and is not presented as carrier survival.
+
+### Common compound scoring and calibration
+
+Global and local evidence use the same implementation in `compound_score.py`:
+
+- for an average of size `c`, the null latent count is `Binomial(c,p)` and the contributor-conditioned count is `x + Binomial(c-1,p)`;
+- five-person interleaving uses `f(y|x)/5 + 4/5 * ((1-p)f(y|0)+p f(y|1))`;
+- log mixtures use stable log-sum-exp and per-symbol common-max normalization;
+- candidate-independent Gaussian components carry observations, variances, and mixture weights; erased `q0` and `q1` are exactly zero; and
+- hard decoding is separately identified as a calibrated fixed-BSC model, with the v1 signed score retained only as a historical diagnostic.
+
+The conditional Chernoff calculation uses the fixed positive 181-point logspace lambda grid from `1e-6` through `1e3` and
+
+`K(lambda) = sum_j logaddexp(log(1-p_j)+lambda*q_j(0), log(p_j)+lambda*q_j(1))`.
+
+Lambda selection uses only committed evidence and biases before any row score. Every result records `H`, family alpha, selected lambda/grid index, cumulant, active symbols, strict comparison, modeled upper bound, threshold, and the claim limit. Tests compare average/interleave likelihoods to direct formulas, the Chernoff threshold to exact toy enumeration, and cover erased, zero-evidence, extreme-numeric, and all-1,000-row cases.
